I'm getting there with nethack.  I've ascended some times (where "some"
is an integer between 5 and 10 which I can't remember) and I'm well on
my way to another ascension.  It's a fantastic game and I enjoy it
immensely.  I've been playing it for about 3 years in between working
too hard.

Each time I've ascended I've done so with a different character class -
wizard, valkyrie, rogue, samurai and so on - a good mix of thugs and
spellcasters.  I enjoy playing to the strengths of each class.  Being
able to alter your playing style is a key element of the game and one
of the things that makes it consistently challenging and enjoyable.

Reading this newsgroup it is obvious that everyone enjoys the early and
mid-game.  However, YAAPs and YAFAPs tend to skip over the late game.
And there is general agreement that Gehennom is boring.  I think that
at least part of the reason that the late game is boring is that there
isn't enough differentiation between the character classes in the late
game.

In the early game it's great - all the classes very different.  But
then the early and mid game is spent trying to become the ideal,
ascendable character (all stats at or close to 18, speed boots, dragon
scale mail, a source of reflection and MR, certain key bits of
equipment, key spellbooks, etc).  This "identikit" character looks and
acts pretty much the same regardless of which class you started out as.
 An ascended Wizard might look very similar to an ascended Samurai if
you just look at the YAAP dump.

So I think that the problem is this: once you have managed to get a
character to a point where they are ascendable (ie you are passing from
the mid-game into the late-game), that character will look pretty much
like all the other characters that get to that point.  And that's
pretty boring - the differences that made the early game so engaging
are gone.

Perhaps I'm wrong and this isn't a problem.  It just strikes me as a
shame that the game always degenerates into hacking your way through
hordes of monsters in order to get to the end.

I don't have any solutions for this, but I'll be very interested to
read everyone's responses.

